// =============================================================================
// AI usage metering + tenant billing
//
// The vocabulary of the append-only `AiUsageEvent` ledger, the effective-dated
// `AiPriceBook`, and the invoice plane built on top of them. See
// `usage-ledger.prisma` / `billing.prisma` for the models and

// decisions (D2, D10–D17) each value encodes.
// =============================================================================

// The metered billing buckets. This is the BILLING taxonomy, not the service
// topology: guardrail and harness LLM calls are metered under `LLM`
// (with a distinguishing `operation`) because they are the same COGS unit — D16
// keeps them out of the tenant's invoice, not out of the ledger.
//
// TASK-959 added the last two, and neither is an inference capability. That is
// the point: the invoice engine iterates THIS enum, so a measure that has no
// member here has no allowance, no invoice line and no place on the
// consumption screen. A parallel storage plane would be a second invoice
// engine.
enum AiCapability {
  STT
  LLM
  NLP
  TTS
  EMBEDDING
  // Storage held, not inference performed — the nightly `storage.snapshot`
  // rows (one per tenant, per storage class, per UTC day). A capability rather
  // than a unit hung off an existing one because a tenant's storage bill must
  // be separable from its STT bill at every level the engine aggregates.
  STORAGE
  // The DURABLE-FUNCTION SERVER's own compute — the CPU `hope-harness-worker`
  // burns orchestrating a workflow run (node activities, sensors, claim-check
  // I/O, run-event emission), as opposed to the inference legs that run calls.
  // Its own capability because a run's worker CPU is neither STT nor LLM, and
  // giving it one is what lets the engine grant it an allowance and an invoice
  // line without touching the inference capabilities.
  WORKFLOW

  @@schema("core")
}

// The unit a ledger row is denominated in. One row per unit kind, all sharing a
// `requestId` — a single LLM call therefore lands as several rows (input,
// output, cache-read, …) rather than one wide row, so a new unit never needs a
// migration and provider-specific token kinds stay separable.
//
// Trap encoded here: cache and reasoning tokens are SEPARATE units
// because providers disagree about whether they are already included in
// `input_tokens` (Anthropic/Bedrock exclude, OpenAI/Gemini include). Normalizing
// into distinct rows is what makes the two reconcilable.
enum AiUsageUnit {
  INPUT_TOKEN
  OUTPUT_TOKEN
  CACHE_READ_TOKEN
  CACHE_WRITE_TOKEN
  REASONING_TOKEN
  // Decoded audio processed (batch) or streamed (realtime).
  AUDIO_SECOND
  // Socket open→close wall time for a streaming STT session. Both are recorded;
  // quota/billing uses SESSION_SECOND (OQ1).
  SESSION_SECOND
  // TTS input, counted as Unicode CODE POINTS — no CJK/Indic double-counting.
  CHARACTER
  // NLP text units (chars/100), consultation-batched.
  TEXT_UNIT
  REQUEST
  // Recorded alongside tokens for self-hosted inference: GPU-time is the
  // cost-truth unit, tokens remain the sell unit (D4).
  GPU_SECOND
  // ── TASK-959 — compute, network and storage ────────────────────────────
  //
  // `GPU_SECOND` and `CPU_SECOND` are OCCUPANCY seconds: the wall-clock time a
  // request held the model on that device. They are NOT scheduler CPU cycles
  // or DCGM SM-active time, and they cannot be: LM Studio runs 8 decode slots
  // per replica, vLLM batches continuously, STT's scheduler batches utterances
  // across sessions, and the cluster time-slices two cards into six units — so
  // summing per-request wall-clock over-counts physical device time by a
  // concurrency factor no service records. Occupancy is what every service
  // already measures, it is what the tenant actually occupied, and the price
  // book absorbs the concurrency instead: a provider's COST row is the
  // device-hour rate divided by its parallel slots. Physical truth for COGS is
  // a MONTHLY reconciliation against the DCGM exporter, never a per-request
  // join (D-2).
  //
  // The unit is chosen by `attributesJson.device`: `cuda`/`mps` → GPU_SECOND,
  // `cpu` → CPU_SECOND.
  CPU_SECOND
  // Third-party network consumption, split into TWO units rather than one unit
  // plus a direction attribute, so direction stays a dimension of the ROW: the
  // rollup is keyed on `unit`, so a `direction` attribute would be invisible to
  // every aggregate and an egress figure could never be read off the rollup at
  // all. COST is 0 today (no vendor or tunnel bills per byte); the rows exist
  // for visibility and capacity planning until a SELL decision (D-3).
  EGRESS_BYTE
  INGRESS_BYTE
  // Storage is a STOCK, and every other unit here is a flow. The GIGABYTE-DAY is
  // what makes the two commensurable: one row per (tenant, class) per UTC day,
  // quantity = bytes held at the snapshot ÷ 1e9, so the existing daily rollup
  // and the existing invoice summation handle storage with no special case.
  // GB-month for an invoice line is then Σ GB-days ÷ days in the period —
  // arithmetic in the engine, not a second grain in the ledger (D-4).
  //
  // GIGABYTES, NOT BYTES, and the reason is the price column. `unitPriceMicros`
  // is an INTEGER number of micros per one unit, so the smallest rate it can
  // express is 1µ. A byte-day at amortised disk (~$0.08/GB-month) costs
  // ~0.0000027µ — six orders of magnitude under that floor — so a byte-day unit
  // could only ever be priced at 0 (storage free forever) or at 1µ (a GB-month
  // at ~$30,000). The gigabyte-day lands the same rate at ~2,667µ, comfortably
  // inside integer micros, and it is also the unit the SELL side wants: a
  // GB-month is a plain sum of GB-days.
  //
  // Resolution is bounded by the QUANTITY column, not the unit: `quantity` is
  // `Decimal(24, 6)`, so six decimal places of a gigabyte = 1e-6 GB = 1 KB.
  // Anything a tenant stores rounds to the nearest kilobyte-day, which is finer
  // than any storage backend reports and far finer than a billing period needs.
  STORAGE_GB_DAY

  @@schema("core")
}

